abstract: >-
  Course website for the "DH Lab", the hands-on companion to "Introduction to Digital
  Humanities" at the University of Bern (Walter Benjamin Kolleg / Digital Humanities). Over
  fourteen sessions in the autumn semester the Lab builds a practical digital research workflow:
  the command line and file systems, Markdown, Git and GitHub, VSCodium, relational data
  modelling with SQLite, the cultural heritage ontologies RiC-O, LRM and CIDOC-CRM, APIs
  including the Zotero API and IIIF, TEI encoding, publication with Omeka and GitHub Pages,
  notebooks and topic modelling, data cleaning and reconciliation with OpenRefine, handwritten
  text recognition with eScriptorium, and the applied use of multimodal large language models.
  The Lab also hosts the programme's introductions, consultations and final-year presentations.
